# =============================================================================
# Google Meet / Google Calendar — env-var template
# =============================================================================
# This file is the ONLY place that documents the env vars the Google Meet
# integration reads. When you're configuring the integration, copy these
# five lines into your real `.env` (locally) and `.env.production` (on
# Hostinger), then fill in the values.
#
# Where the values come from
#   GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID         · Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services
#                                      → Credentials → your Web app client
#   GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET     · Same page as Client ID
#   GOOGLE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN     · Output of the one-time bootstrap:
#                                      node scripts/google-oauth-bootstrap.js
#   GOOGLE_CALENDAR_HOST_EMAIL     · The Google account that owns the
#                                      calendar (also the meeting organizer)
#   GOOGLE_CALENDAR_ID             · "primary" (your main calendar) or a
#                                      specific calendar id like
#                                      abcd1234@group.calendar.google.com
#   GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI      · Used ONLY by the bootstrap script. Leave
#                                      as the default unless you have a
#                                      reason to change it.
#
# Full step-by-step setup walkthrough (Cloud Console → consent screen →
# credentials → refresh-token bootstrap → production deploy):
#
#   docs/google-meet-setup.md
#
# Graceful fallback
#   If any of these is blank or unset, the booking flow silently falls back
#   to Jitsi (https://meet.jit.si/...) and the rest of the system works
#   identically. So you can leave them blank in dev until you're ready.
# =============================================================================

# ── Required for Google Meet auto-links ─────────────────────────────────────
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN=
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_HOST_EMAIL=hello@mustaphaukizuru.com

# ── Optional (sensible defaults) ────────────────────────────────────────────
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_ID=primary
GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5000/api/v1/admin/google/oauth-callback

# =============================================================================
# QUICK REFERENCE — minimum steps once you have a Google Cloud project ready
# =============================================================================
#
# 1. Locally — paste CLIENT_ID and CLIENT_SECRET into D:\mustaphaukizuru-repo\.env
#    (the bootstrap reads from .env). Save.
#
# 2. Locally — run:
#       cd D:\mustaphaukizuru-repo
#       node scripts/google-oauth-bootstrap.js
#    Follow the printed URL → sign in as hello@mustaphaukizuru.com → Allow.
#    Paste the redirect URL back into the terminal.
#    Copy the printed REFRESH_TOKEN.
#
# 3. Locally — paste the REFRESH_TOKEN into the same .env. Restart `npm run dev`.
#    Bookings now create Meet links via your Google account.
#
# 4. Production — SSH into Hostinger:
#       cd ~/htdocs
#       nano .env.production
#    Add all five Google variables (same values as local). Save.
#       pm2 restart mustaphaukizuru --update-env
#
# 5. Production — seed the new email template (rescheduled notice):
#       npm run seed:email
#    (Idempotent — safe to re-run any time.)
#
# 6. Test — book a consultation on https://mustaphaukizuru.com/book-consultation.
#    Within ~5 seconds the client should receive (a) the native Google
#    Calendar invite and (b) the branded confirmation email.
#
# =============================================================================
